    {
      "id": "AM4_Q2_X4",
      "difficulty": "X",
      "skill_tags": [
        "tough_parameter"
      ],
      "problem_text": "If (x-1)^2 is a factor of x^3+ax^2+bx+c, find a,b,c.",
      "answer": "a=-3,b=3,c=-1",
      "solution": "If (x-1)^2 is a factor, then P(1)=0 and P'(1)=0. P(1)=1+a+b+c=0 => a+b+c=-1. P'(x)=3x^2+2ax+b, P'(1)=3+2a+b=0 => 2a+b=-3. Since (x-1)^2 is factor, the remaining factor is (x+c) (since constant term is c and product of roots is -c). x^3+ax^2+bx+c=(x-1)^2(x+d)=(x^2-2x+1)(x+d)=x^3+(d-2)x^2+(1-2d)x+d. So a=d-2, b=1-2d, c=d. From a+b+c=-1: (d-2)+(1-2d)+d=-1 => d-2+1-2d+d=-1 => 0d-1=-1 \u2713. And 2a+b=-3: 2(d-2)+(1-2d)=2d-4+1-2d=-3 \u2713. So any d works? Let's check P(1)=1+a+b+c=1+(d-2)+(1-2d)+d=1+d-2+1-2d+d=0 \u2713. So family: a=d-2, b=1-2d, c=d for any d. E.g. d=-1 => a=-3,b=3,c=-1",
      "marks": 4
    }
